<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Lament · 1866 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A spiritual first sung by enslaved African Americans, telling of sorrow that only God sees and of faith that holds on.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Enslaved African Americans and the freedmen of the South</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>South Carolina, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>originated during slavery; first published April 1866</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional African-American spiritual (first published 1866))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobody-knows-the-trouble-ive-seen">Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen</a>&quot; is an African-American spiritual that took shape during the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> but was not published until after emancipation. It first appeared in print, as &quot;Nobody Knows&quot;, with seven verses, in April 1866 in The Child at Home, a paper of the American Tract Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. The paper called it a specimen of the hymns sung by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> of the South, sent in by a teacher who had been working for some time at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charleston-south-carolina">Charleston, South Carolina</a>, among the formerly enslaved. In May 1866 The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Freedmen's Bulletin reported it being sung at a school near Beaufort, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>.</p><p>In 1867 it was printed with four verses, as &quot;Nobody Knows The Trouble I've Had&quot;, in Slave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; Hugo Frey used this version in his collection Famous Negro Spirituals of 1924. The Jubilee Singers sang a song with a similar chorus but a different tune and words, first published in 1872. In the earliest known versions, and most often in American church hymnals, the second line of the chorus turns from the singer's private sorrow to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as the one witness of it; later versions changed that line.</p><p>From the late 19th century African-American music entered the concert hall in arrangements by Black composers such as Samuel Coleridge-Taylor, Henry Thacker Burleigh and J. Rosamond Johnson. Johnson arranged this spiritual for voice and piano in 1917, while he directed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Music School Settlement for Colored People, and for violin and piano in 1919 at the suggestion of the violinist Maud Powell, the first white solo concert artist to perform classical arrangements of spirituals; she played it that autumn and died in November. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> Price quoted it in her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> Suite of 1934, and Michael Tippett made it one of the five spirituals in his oratorio A Child of Our Time, first performed in 1944.</p><p>The contralto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marian-anderson">Marian Anderson</a> had her first successful recording with the song, on the Victor label, in 1925. It has since been recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahalia-jackson">Mahalia Jackson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-armstrong">Louis Armstrong</a>, Lena Horne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a>, Harry James, Sam Cooke and many others; in 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> Lee, the first Indigenous Australian artist to record blues songs, included it on her album. It has been sung in many films, among them the 1944 race film Go Down, Death! and Roberto Rossellini's Paisan (1946), where an African American soldier sings it to an Italian boy. In June 2026 CBS News named it among the 250 essential American songs of the past 250 years.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of private grief and endurance: the singer has known troubles that no one around them sees or shares, and turns to God as the one who knows them. The mood is sorrowful but steady, with a turn towards praise in the earliest versions.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nobody knows the trouble I've been seen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nobody knows my sorrow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nobody knows the trouble I've seen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Glory hallelujah!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sometimes I'm up, sometimes I'm down</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, yes, Lord</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sometimes I'm almost to the ground</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, yes, Lord</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Although you see me going 'long so</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, yes, Lord</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I have my trials here below</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, yes, Lord</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nobody knows the trouble I've been through</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nobody knows but Jesus</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nobody knows the trouble I've seen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Glory hallelujah!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>If you get there before I do</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, yes, Lord</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Tell all-a my friends I'm coming to Heaven!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, yes, Lord</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobody_Knows_the_Trouble_I've_Seen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="LNLEQU1zLuc" data-title="Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen: Marian Anderson"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Marian Anderson</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Marian Anderson - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNLEQU1zLuc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Famous Negro Spirituals (Hugo Frey, arrangement)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1924</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement of the 1867 version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement for voice and piano (J. Rosamond Johnson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement for violin and piano (J. Rosamond Johnson, for Maud Powell)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1919</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mississippi River Suite (Florence Price)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1934</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Child of Our Time (Michael Tippett)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">one of five spirituals included in the oratorio</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nobody (Ry Cooder, on the album Jazz)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1978</span><span class="sg-chain-h">opening couplet based on the song</span></li></ul></section>
